      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense but i'm having trouble visualizing it. like, how do we know the air actually deflects downward? is there a specific way to think about the air hitting the wing that makes the newton part easier to grasp?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I understand—visualizing these principles can be challenging. To help you better understand the downward deflection of air, let's break it down visually. Picture climbing a smooth, rolling hill that's sloping down at an angle. As you roll down your knees, you push the ground away with your hands and feet, creating a reactionary force that keeps you moving downhill.\n\nNow, envision the hill has bumps that you can jump over as you go, but it also has dips. As you approach these dips, imagine you're crouching low, - to slide down. As you hit just the bottom of these dips and slide down, it's not just gravity pulling you downward; it's also the curved surface - the dips - that guides and carries you across them. You're pushed along by the sides as you steer around.\n\nIn your wing example, as air hits the wing at the leading edge and moves over the upper curved surface, it's guided downward by the wing's design. This resemblance to the curvature of a hill lets the wing deflect the air downward and sideways. This creates an area of low pressure above the wing, leading to lift. You need to visualize the wing as a gently curving surface that directs the air downward, much like how a hill or",
